window.IG_COURSE=[{"week":1,"title":"Replicator Equations and Scalar Dynamics","subtitle":"Competition, proportions, and equilibria","summary":"Connect population proportions, phase lines, vector-field graphs, and time-dependent solutions in a visual study of competition.","reading":"readings/week01.html","demo":"demos/week01.html","lab":["A mixed population begins with 48 individuals of type A and 72 of type B. Later it contains 90 of type A and 180 of type B. Compute the total size and the proportion of A at each time. Did the number of A individuals increase? Did A's proportion increase? Explain why those conclusions differ.","Suppose \\(N_A'=f_A N_A\\) and \\(N_B'=f_B N_B\\). Starting with \\(x=N_A/(N_A+N_B)\\), use the quotient rule to derive \\(x'=x(1-x)(f_A-f_B)\\). Then suppose the fitness advantage is the constant \\(f_A-f_B=1.5\\). Identify the equilibria and sketch a phase line on \\([0,1]\\).","For \\(x'=x(1-x)(3-4x)\\): (a) find all equilibria in \\([0,1]\\); (b) calculate the signs of \\(x'\\) at \\(x=0.25,\\;0.60,\\;0.90\\); (c) draw a phase line with arrows and classify each equilibrium; (d) explain the population meaning of the stable equilibrium.","Open the synchronized demo. Select \"Stable coexistence,\" set \\(x(0)=0.20\\), and advance to \\(t=4\\). Record \\(x(t)\\) and \\(x'(t)\\), then explain how these values appear in the phase line, vector-field graph, and solution graph. Repeat with \\(x(0)=0.90\\). Use the three pictures to explain why both solutions approach the same equilibrium.","Select \"Bistability (threshold)\" in the demo. Run from \\(x(0)=0.30\\) and \\(x(0)=0.70\\). Record their long-term trends. Then examine \\(x'=x(1-x)(2-5x)\\): find the interior equilibrium, determine stability using a sign chart, and calculate \\(F'(x^*)\\). Explain how the local derivative relates to what you see in the solution curve."],"quiz":[["A population contains 20 A individuals and 30 B individuals. What is the proportion \\(x\\) of type A?",["\\(\\frac25\\)","\\(\\frac23\\)","\\(\\frac35\\)","\\(\\frac12\\)"],0,"Divide the count of A by the total: 20/(20+30)=2/5."],["For \\(0<x<1\\), if \\(f_A>f_B\\), what happens to the proportion of A?",["It decreases.","It increases.","It stays constant.","It becomes larger than 1."],1,"Inside the interval x(1−x)>0, so the sign of x′ is the sign of the fitness difference."],["For \\(x'=2x(1-x)\\), which equilibrium attracts all interior initial conditions?",["x=0","x=1","x=1/2","There is no equilibrium."],1,"The derivative is positive throughout the interior, so trajectories move toward 1."],["What are the equilibria of \\(x'=x(1-x)(3-4x)\\)?",["\\(0,\\;\\frac12,\\;1\\)","\\(0,\\;\\frac34,\\;1\\)","\\(\\frac14,\\;\\frac34\\)","\\(0,\\;1\\) only"],1,"Set each of the three factors equal to zero."],["At \\(x=0.90\\) for \\(x'=x(1-x)(3-4x)\\), what is the sign of \\(x'\\)?",["Positive","Zero","Negative","Cannot be determined"],2,"The factors x and 1−x are positive, while 3−4(0.90) is negative."],["Which graph has time on its horizontal axis?",["The graph of \\(F(x)=x'\\) against x","The solution graph \\(x(t)\\)","The phase line","The graph of the two fitness functions versus x"],1,"A solution graph follows one initial condition through time. The vector-field graph uses state x on the horizontal axis."],["For \\(F(x)=x(1-x)(3-4x)\\), what is \\(F'(3/4)\\)?",["\\(-\\frac34\\)","\\(\\frac34\\)","0","3"],0,"At the interior root, F′=x(1−x)(−4)=−(3/4)(1/4)4=−3/4."]],"sections":[["From populations to proportions","Let \\(N_A(t)\\) and \\(N_B(t)\\) denote two populations, growing according to \\(N_A'=f_A N_A\\) and \\(N_B'=f_B N_B\\). Their growth rates can depend on population composition. Define the fraction \\(x=N_A/(N_A+N_B)\\). By the quotient rule,\n\\[\nx'=\\frac{N_A'N_B-N_AN_B'}{(N_A+N_B)^2}\n=x(1-x)(f_A-f_B).\n\\]\nThe absolute population size disappears: this equation describes relative abundance. It is called the two-strategy replicator equation."],["A familiar special case","If the fitness difference is a positive constant \\(k\\), then \\(x'=kx(1-x)\\). Its equilibria are \\(0\\) and \\(1\\), and every solution initially between them increases toward \\(1\\). Separation of variables gives\n\\[\nx(t)=\\frac{1}{1+\\bigl((1-x_0)/x_0\\bigr)e^{-kt}}.\n\\]\nIt has the logistic form, but here \\(1\\) means complete dominance by type A, not a carrying capacity."],["Frequency-dependent fitness","Suppose \\(f_A=3-2x\\) and \\(f_B=2x\\). Then \\(x'=x(1-x)(3-4x)\\). The zeros are \\(0,\\;3/4,\\;1\\). For \\(0<x<3/4\\), the derivative is positive; for \\(3/4<x<1\\), it is negative. Hence the interior equilibrium \\(x^*=3/4\\) attracts interior trajectories. With \\(F(x)=3x-7x^2+4x^3\\), we obtain \\(F'(3/4)=-3/4\\), so nearby perturbations satisfy approximately \\(u'=-3u/4\\)."]],"key":"x'=x(1-x)(f_A-f_B)","example":"For \\(x'=x(1-x)(2-5x)\\), the equilibria are \\(0,2/5,1\\). The sign of \\(x'\\) is positive below \\(2/5\\) and negative above it, so \\(2/5\\) is asymptotically stable for interior initial conditions.","readingPath":"readings/week01.html","demoPath":"demos/week01.html","labPath":"labs/week01.html","quizPath":"quizzes/week01.html"},{"week":2,"title":"Payoff Matrices and Stability","subtitle":"From interactions to the entire phase portrait","summary":"Use a 2×2 payoff matrix to derive expected fitness, predict invasion and stability, and investigate bifurcations.","reading":"readings/week02.html","demo":"demos/week02.html","lab":["A population uses strategies A and B with payoff matrix \\(M=\\left(\\begin{smallmatrix}2&4\\\\3&1\\end{smallmatrix}\\right)\\). (a) Write \\(f_A(x)\\) and \\(f_B(x)\\) as weighted averages and simplify. (b) Evaluate both at \\(x=1/4\\) and \\(x=9/10\\). (c) From these two states, which direction does A’s proportion move? (d) Explain how the fitness-line intersection predicts an equilibrium.","Derive the equation \\(x'=x(1-x)\\left[(b-d)+(a-b-c+d)x\\right]\\). For \\(M=\\left(\\begin{smallmatrix}4&3\\\\2&1\\end{smallmatrix}\\right)\\), compute the fitness difference, draw a phase line, and describe the long-term population composition from any interior initial state. Confirm using the demo’s A-dominance preset.","Consider the coordination matrix \\(M=\\left(\\begin{smallmatrix}4&1\\\\2&3\\end{smallmatrix}\\right)\\). (a) Find the interior equilibrium and classify all equilibria on \\([0,1]\\). (b) Use the demo with initial proportions \\(x(0)=0.35\\) and \\(x(0)=0.65\\). (c) Explain what separates their long-term outcomes. (d) Sketch the two solution curves on the same axes.","Fix \\(b=4,\\;c=3,\\;d=1\\) while changing \\(a\\) from 2 to 4. Use the demo’s \\(a\\)-slider. At \\(a=2,\\;2.8,\\;3,\\;3.5\\), record the equilibrium locations and their stability. Determine algebraically the parameter at which stable coexistence disappears into the boundary. Explain what the phase line looks like at the critical parameter.","Begin with \\(M=\\left(\\begin{smallmatrix}2&4\\\\3&1\\end{smallmatrix}\\right)\\). Use the demo to compare the original model with (i) the matrix formed by adding 1 to every payoff and (ii) the matrix formed by multiplying every payoff by 2. Show algebraically which transformation leaves the differential equation unchanged. For the other transformation, identify what changes and what remains the same about the trajectories."],"quiz":[["For \\(M=\\left(\\begin{smallmatrix}a&b\\\\c&d\\end{smallmatrix}\\right)\\), the expected fitness of strategy A is",["\\(ax+b(1-x)\\)","\\(cx+d(1-x)\\)","\\(a+b\\)","\\(ax+cx\\)"],0,"Weight A's payoffs against A and B by their frequencies x and 1−x."],["For \\(M=\\left(\\begin{smallmatrix}2&4\\\\3&1\\end{smallmatrix}\\right)\\), the difference \\(f_A-f_B\\) equals",["\\(1-2x\\)","\\(3-4x\\)","\\(4x-3\\)","\\(2x+1\\)"],1,"Here f_A=4−2x and f_B=1+2x; subtract."],["When A is very rare, what determines whether its proportion initially increases?",["\\(a>c\\)","\\(a>b\\)","\\(b>d\\)","\\(c>d\\)"],2,"At x=0 the fitness difference is b−d."],["Which endpoint signs indicate stable interior coexistence?",["\\(\\Delta(0)>0,\\;\\Delta(1)<0\\)","\\(\\Delta(0)<0,\\;\\Delta(1)>0\\)","\\(\\Delta(0)>0,\\;\\Delta(1)>0\\)","\\(\\Delta(0)<0,\\;\\Delta(1)<0\\)"],0,"The advantage must change from positive on the left to negative on the right."],["For the coordination matrix \\(M=\\left(\\begin{smallmatrix}4&1\\\\2&3\\end{smallmatrix}\\right)\\), the interior equilibrium is",["\\(x^*=3/4\\), stable","\\(x^*=1/2\\), stable","\\(x^*=1/2\\), unstable","\\(x^*=1/4\\), unstable"],2,"The fitness difference is −2+4x. Its sign changes from negative to positive at 1/2."],["For an interior equilibrium \\(p\\) of \\(F(x)=x(1-x)(\\alpha+\\beta x)\\), which expression equals \\(F'(p)\\)?",["\\(p(1-p)\\beta\\)","\\(\\alpha+\\beta\\)","\\(p(1-p)\\alpha\\)","\\(0\\) always"],0,"Differentiate the product. At p, the factor α+βp is zero."],["What happens if the same constant is added to every entry of the payoff matrix?",["Both strategies immediately disappear.","The interior equilibrium always moves to 1/2.","The entire replicator equation stays unchanged.","The direction of every trajectory reverses."],2,"Each expected fitness increases by the same amount, so their difference is unchanged."]],"sections":[["Interactions become expected fitness","With proportion \\(x\\) of strategy A and payoff matrix \\(M=\\begin{pmatrix}a&b\\\\c&d\\end{pmatrix}\\), random encounters give expected fitness \\(f_A(x)=ax+b(1-x)\\) and \\(f_B(x)=cx+d(1-x)\\). Row entries are the payoffs received by the corresponding strategy, while columns identify opponents. The replicator equation remains \\(x'=x(1-x)(f_A-f_B)\\)."],["The difference is a line","Subtracting gives \\(\\Delta(x)=f_A-f_B=(b-d)+(a-b-c+d)x\\), so \\(x'=x(1-x)\\Delta(x)\\). The endpoint values are \\(\\Delta(0)=b-d\\) and \\(\\Delta(1)=a-c\\). A rare type A grows in frequency when \\(b>d\\); a rare type B grows in frequency when \\(c>a\\). These signs determine the arrows on the phase line."],["Four qualitative cases","If \\(\\Delta\\) is positive throughout \\((0,1)\\), A dominates; if negative, B dominates. If it changes from positive to negative, an interior equilibrium attracts, giving stable coexistence. If it changes from negative to positive, an unstable interior equilibrium separates two basins of attraction, called bistability or coordination. If \\(\\Delta\\) vanishes at an endpoint, use a sign chart; if it vanishes everywhere, every state is stationary."],["Linearization and parameters","Write \\(\\alpha=b-d\\), \\(\\beta=a-b-c+d\\), so \\(F(x)=x(1-x)(\\alpha+\\beta x)\\). At an interior equilibrium \\(p=-\\alpha/\\beta\\), \\(F'(p)=p(1-p)\\beta\\). Also \\(F'(0)=b-d\\) and \\(F'(1)=c-a\\). For \\(M=\\left(\\begin{smallmatrix}a&4\\\\3&1\\end{smallmatrix}\\right)\\), \\(\\Delta(x)=3+(a-6)x\\), and the interior attracting state reaches \\(x=1\\) when \\(a=3\\). This is a bifurcation."]],"key":"x'=x(1-x)\\left[(b-d)+(a-b-c+d)x\\right]","example":"For \\(M=\\left(\\begin{smallmatrix}2&4\\\\3&1\\end{smallmatrix}\\right)\\), the expected fitnesses are \\(f_A=4-2x\\) and \\(f_B=1+2x\\). Hence \\(\\Delta(x)=3-4x\\), with stable coexistence at \\(x^*=3/4\\). By contrast, \\(M=\\left(\\begin{smallmatrix}4&1\\\\2&3\\end{smallmatrix}\\right)\\) gives \\(\\Delta=-2+4x\\) and an unstable threshold \\(x^*=1/2\\).","readingPath":"readings/week02.html","demoPath":"demos/week02.html","labPath":"labs/week02.html","quizPath":"quizzes/week02.html"},{"week":3,"title":"Three Strategies and the Probability Simplex","subtitle":"From the interval to a triangle","summary":"Derive a three-strategy replicator system and explore invariant faces, cyclic competition, and planar stability.","reading":"readings/week03.html","demo":"demos/week03.html","lab":["A population has proportions \\(\\mathbf p=(0.50,0.30,0.20)\\). (a) Explain why it is a point in the triangle \\(\\Delta^2\\). (b) Identify the missing strategy along each of the three edges. (c) Give an example of a tangent velocity vector whose coordinates sum to zero, and describe which strategies gain and lose share. (d) In the demo, drag the initial point to a new interior location and estimate its three proportions.","Derive the three-strategy replicator equation. Begin with \\(N_i'=f_iN_i\\), set \\(p_i=N_i/N\\), and calculate \\(N'/N\\). Then, for \\(\\mathbf p=(0.2,0.3,0.5)\\) and \\(\\mathbf f=(3,1,2)\\), compute \\(\\overline f\\) and \\(\\mathbf p'\\). Verify that the velocity is tangent to the simplex.","Consider the cyclic payoff model with \\(s=1\\). Start with \\(\\mathbf p(0)=(0.4,0.6,0)\\), exactly on the AB edge. (a) Write the fitnesses on that edge. (b) Reduce the system to an equation for \\(p_A\\) and predict which pure state attracts the edge trajectory. (c) Observe this in the simplex demo. (d) Repeat with \\(\\mathbf p(0)=(0.4,0.59,0.01)\\). Why can the second trajectory explore the interior while the first cannot?","Set the cyclic loss magnitude to \\(s=0.7\\), then \\(s=1\\), then \\(s=1.3\\), using the same initial point \\((0.55,0.25,0.20)\\). For each model, sketch the trajectory on the triangle, describe the time series, and record how \\(P=p_Ap_Bp_C\\) changes. Derive \\(\\frac{d}{dt}\\ln P=(1-s)(1-3Q)\\), where \\(Q=p_Ap_B+p_Bp_C+p_Cp_A\\), and use it to explain all three observations.","Use \\(x=p_A\\) and \\(y=p_B\\), so \\(p_C=1-x-y\\). The reading gives the Jacobian at the central equilibrium for the cyclic model: \\(J_s=\\frac13\\left(\\begin{smallmatrix}s&s+1\\\\-(s+1)&-1\\end{smallmatrix}\\right)\\). (a) Compute its trace and determinant. (b) Classify the equilibrium for \\(s=0.7,\\;1,\\;1.3\\) using the eigenvalues. (c) Compare the Cartesian phase-plane view to the barycentric triangle view in the demo. Explain why they describe the same planar system."],"quiz":[["Which point lies in the standard three-strategy probability simplex?",["\\((0.2,0.3,0.5)\\)","\\((0.4,0.4,0.4)\\)","\\((-0.1,0.5,0.6)\\)","\\((1,1,0)\\)"],0,"All components must be nonnegative and their sum must equal 1."],["How many independent coordinates are needed to describe a three-strategy probability vector?",["One","Two","Three","Four"],1,"Normalization imposes one independent linear constraint on three coordinates."],["If \\(\\mathbf p=(0.2,0.3,0.5)\\) and \\(\\mathbf f=(3,1,2)\\), what is the mean fitness?",["1.0","1.5","1.9","2.5"],2,"Compute the weighted average: 0.2·3+0.3·1+0.5·2=1.9."],["Under replicator dynamics, the sum \\(p_A'+p_B'+p_C'\\) is",["Equal to 1","Equal to mean fitness","Equal to 0","Always negative"],2,"The derivative of p_A+p_B+p_C=1 is 0, and the replicator terms cancel."],["If \\(p_C(0)=0\\), what happens to C under the smooth replicator equation?",["Its proportion becomes positive immediately","Its proportion stays zero","The state leaves the simplex","Both remaining proportions become zero"],1,"The right-hand side of C's equation has a factor p_C, so the edge is invariant."],["For \\(M_s=\\left(\\begin{smallmatrix}0&1&-s\\\\-s&0&1\\\\1&-s&0\\end{smallmatrix}\\right)\\), which state is the interior equilibrium?",["\\((1,0,0)\\)","\\((1/2,1/2,0)\\)","\\((1/3,1/3,1/3)\\)","\\((0,0,0)\\)"],2,"At the uniform composition, all three strategies have equal fitness."],["For the balanced cyclic model \\(s=1\\), the product \\(p_Ap_Bp_C\\) along a nonstationary interior trajectory is",["Strictly increasing","Strictly decreasing","Constant","Always zero"],2,"The logarithmic derivative contains the factor 1−s and therefore vanishes when s=1."],["For \\(s=0.7\\), trajectories near the central equilibrium generally",["Spiral outward","Spiral inward","Remain stationary at every point","Leave the triangle in finite time"],1,"The real part of the central Jacobian eigenvalues is (s−1)/6<0, and the product of proportions increases."]],"sections":[["The simplex as state space","A probability state is \\(\\mathbf p=(p_A,p_B,p_C)\\) with nonnegative coordinates summing to 1. Its space is a triangle, \\(\\Delta^2\\). The triangle has two independent coordinates because \\(p_C=1-p_A-p_B\\); its vertices are pure states and its edges are two-strategy mixtures."],["The three-strategy system","If \\(N_i'=f_iN_i\\), then differentiating \\(p_i=N_i/\\sum_jN_j\\) gives \\(p_i'=p_i(f_i-\\overline f)\\), where \\(\\overline f=\\sum_jp_jf_j\\). On the simplex, \\(\\sum_i p_i'=0\\), so velocities are tangent to the plane of normalized states. If \\(p_k=0\\), then \\(p_k'=0\\); each edge is invariant under smooth replicator dynamics."],["Cyclic competition and circulation","For \\(M_s=\\left(\\begin{smallmatrix}0&1&-s\\\\-s&0&1\\\\1&-s&0\\end{smallmatrix}\\right)\\), the fitness vector is \\(M_s\\mathbf p\\), and \\(\\mathbf p^*=(1/3,1/3,1/3)\\) is an equilibrium. When \\(s=1\\), interior trajectories are closed orbits around that center. If \\(s<1\\), the center attracts; if \\(s>1\\), it repels and trajectories wind outward toward the boundary."],["A quantity that explains stability","For positive proportions, let \\(P=p_Ap_Bp_C\\) and \\(Q=p_Ap_B+p_Bp_C+p_Cp_A\\). The cyclic model obeys \\(\\frac{d}{dt}\\ln P=(1-s)(1-3Q)\\), and \\(Q\\leq1/3\\). Thus \\(P\\) increases for \\(s<1\\), is constant for \\(s=1\\), and decreases for \\(s>1\\) away from the central equilibrium. In the coordinates \\(x=p_A,y=p_B\\), the central Jacobian has eigenvalues \\(\\lambda_\\pm=(s-1)/6\\pm i\\sqrt3(s+1)/6\\)."]],"key":"p_i'=p_i(f_i-\\overline f),\\qquad \\overline f=\\sum_jp_jf_j","example":"For \\(\\mathbf p=(0.2,0.3,0.5)\\) and \\(\\mathbf f=(3,1,2)\\), mean fitness is \\(1.9\\). Hence \\(\\mathbf p'=(0.22,-0.27,0.05)\\), whose coordinates sum to zero. The state remains on the normalized probability plane.","readingPath":"readings/week03.html","demoPath":"demos/week03.html","labPath":"labs/week03.html","quizPath":"quizzes/week03.html"},{"week":4,"title":"Probability Distributions as States","subtitle":"Random variables, expectation, variance, and curves of distributions","summary":"Interpret simplex points as probability laws, explore Bernoulli and categorical families, and compare exact probabilities with samples.","reading":"readings/week04.html","demo":"demos/week04.html","lab":["A measuring device reports outcomes A, B, or C with probabilities \\((1/2,1/3,1/6)\\). The corresponding readings are \\(0,1,3\\). (a) Verify that the probabilities define a distribution. (b) Draw a probability bar graph with properly labeled numerical outcomes. (c) Calculate the mean and variance using the definitions, and interpret why the mean need not be a possible reading.","Let \\(X\\) have the Bernoulli distribution \\(P(X=1)=p\\). (a) Starting from \\(E[X]\\) and \\(E[X^2]\\), derive \\(E[X]=p\\) and \\(\\operatorname{Var}(X)=p(1-p)\\). (b) Find the maximum variance using calculus. (c) In the demo, compare \\(p=0.1,0.5,0.9\\), recording the two probabilities, mean, and variance for each.","Fix numerical outcomes \\(z=(0,1,3)\\), and use categorical states \\(P=(0.6,0.3,0.1)\\) and \\(Q=(0.2,0.3,0.5)\\). (a) Calculate the expectation and variance for P and Q. (b) Define \\(P(t)=(1-t)P+tQ\\) and find its velocity. (c) Use the demo's interpolation mode, observe the trajectory on the triangle, and derive \\(dE_{P(t)}[Z]/dt\\). (d) Does the variance change linearly in t? Verify algebraically.","Select the categorical distribution \\(\\mathbf p=(0.5,1/3,1/6)\\) (or enter decimal approximations). Draw 10 samples with the demo, then 100 more, and then 1000 more without changing the distribution. Record the empirical frequencies after each stage. Explain why the results change between runs, what the empirical frequencies estimate, and why we should not expect exact agreement even after 1000 observations.","Using the categorical model with outcomes \\(z=(0,1,3)\\), set \\(p_A=0.35\\), \\(p_B=0.4\\), and \\(p_C=0.25\\). (a) Write its expectation as \\(\\mu(x,y)=3-3x-2y\\) in coordinates \\(x=p_A,\\ y=p_B\\). (b) Find \\(\\partial\\mu/\\partial x\\) and \\(\\partial\\mu/\\partial y\\). (c) Consider the tangent vector \\(v=(-0.1,0.05,0.05)\\). Show that its coordinates sum to zero, calculate the directional derivative of the expectation, and describe the change in the probability bars."],"quiz":[["A categorical probability distribution must have",["nonnegative probabilities summing to 1","numerical outcomes summing to 1","equal probability for every outcome","exactly two outcomes"],0,"Probabilities are nonnegative and normalized; outcome values may be arbitrary."],["A random observation from a three-category distribution is",["a whole probability vector","one realized outcome, such as B","always the most likely category","the expectation of the distribution"],1,"A model assigns chances; sampling produces one category."],["For \\(z=(0,1,3)\\) and \\(p=(1/2,1/3,1/6)\\), the expectation equals",["\\(\\frac56\\)","\\(\\frac43\\)","\\(\\frac12\\)","\\(\\frac{11}{6}\\)"],0,"Compute 0·1/2 + 1·1/3 + 3·1/6 = 5/6."],["For the same distribution, the variance equals",["\\(\\frac{25}{36}\\)","\\(\\frac{41}{36}\\)","\\(\\frac{11}{6}\\)","\\(\\frac56\\)"],1,"The second moment is 11/6; subtract the square of the mean, 25/36."],["For a Bernoulli random variable with \\(P(X=1)=p\\), its variance is",["\\(p\\)","\\(1-p\\)","\\(p(1-p)\\)","\\(p^2\\)"],2,"Because X²=X on outcomes {0,1}, variance is E[X]−E[X]²."],["At which Bernoulli parameter does variance reach its maximum?",["\\(p=0\\)","\\(p=\\frac14\\)","\\(p=\\frac12\\)","\\(p=1\\)"],2,"Differentiate p(1−p) or complete the square."],["A differentiable categorical distribution \\(\\mathbf p(t)\\) obeys which identity?",["\\(\\sum_i p_i'=1\\)","\\(\\sum_i p_i'=0\\)","\\(p_i'=p_i\\) always","\\(\\sum_i p_i^2=1\\)"],1,"Differentiate the normalization condition ∑ p_i(t)=1."],["When repeatedly sampling independently from a fixed probability distribution, the empirical proportions",["must equal the exact probabilities after 100 samples","usually differ from the probabilities but tend toward them with large sample size","form a vector whose entries sum to 2","are always constant across repeated experiments"],1,"Sample frequencies are random; the law of large numbers describes their convergence as sample size grows."]],"sections":[["A distribution describes probabilities","A categorical distribution is a vector \\(\\mathbf p=(p_1,\\ldots,p_n)\\), where the components are nonnegative and sum to one. It describes the probabilities of possible observations, rather than a particular observed outcome. For three categories, the space of distributions is the simplex \\(\\Delta^2\\)."],["Numerical random variables and moments","If the categories have numerical values \\(z_1,\\ldots,z_n\\), expectation is \\(E_{\\mathbf p}[Z]=\\sum_i z_i p_i\\). Writing \\(\\mu=E[Z]\\), the variance is \\(\\operatorname{Var}(Z)=\\sum_i p_i(z_i-\\mu)^2=\\sum_i z_i^2 p_i-\\mu^2\\). For \\(z=(0,1,3)\\) and \\(\\mathbf p=(1/2,1/3,1/6)\\), we get \\(E[Z]=5/6\\) and \\(\\operatorname{Var}(Z)=41/36\\)."],["Bernoulli distribution family","The Bernoulli family has possible outcomes 0 and 1 with probabilities \\(1-p,p\\). Thus \\(E[X]=p\\) and \\(\\operatorname{Var}(X)=p(1-p)\\), maximized at \\(p=1/2\\). As the parameter changes, the distribution moves along a one-dimensional interval of statistical states."],["Curves and samples in the simplex","A differentiable curve of distributions \\(\\mathbf p(t)\\) satisfies \\(\\sum_i p_i'(t)=0\\). For fixed numerical outcomes, \\(\\frac{d}{dt}E[Z]=\\sum_i z_i p_i'(t)\\). Linear interpolation \\(P(t)=(1-t)P_0+tP_1\\) remains normalized and has constant velocity \\(P_1-P_0\\), but need not solve the replicator equation. For \\(n\\) independent samples, the empirical distribution is \\(\\widehat p_i=n_i/n\\), and typically differs from the underlying \\(\\mathbf p\\)."]],"key":"E_{\\mathbf p}[Z]=\\sum_i p_i z_i,\\qquad \\operatorname{Var}_{\\mathbf p}(Z)=\\sum_i p_i z_i^2-\\left(\\sum_i p_i z_i\\right)^2","example":"For \\(P=(1/2,1/3,1/6)\\) and outcomes \\(z=(0,1,3)\\), the mean is \\(0(1/2)+1(1/3)+3(1/6)=5/6\\). The second moment is \\(1/3+9/6=11/6\\). Thus the variance is \\(11/6-25/36=41/36\\).","readingPath":"readings/week04.html","demoPath":"demos/week04.html","labPath":"labs/week04.html","quizPath":"quizzes/week04.html"},{"week":5,"title":"Entropy, Log-Odds, and Exponential Updating","subtitle":"The bridge from replicator dynamics to information theory","summary":"Measure surprisal and Shannon entropy, transform replicator equations with log-odds, and solve constant-fitness dynamics by exponential normalization.","reading":"readings/week05.html","demo":"demos/week05.html","lab":["For events of probability \\(1/2\\), \\(1/4\\), and \\(1/10\\), compute surprisal in both nats and bits. Draw or describe how \\(I(p)=-\\ln p\\) changes as \\(p\\) moves toward 0 and 1. Then suppose A and B are independent events with probabilities \\(1/2\\) and \\(1/5\\): compute the surprisal of their joint occurrence and verify that it equals the sum of the individual surprisals.","For the Bernoulli family \\(P_p=(p,1-p)\\), write Shannon entropy \\(H(p)\\), calculate \\(H'(p)\\) and \\(H''(p)\\), and determine the location and value of its maximum. Use the demo to compare \\(p=0.1,\\;0.5,\\;0.9\\). Why is uncertainty symmetric between p and 1−p? Convert the maximum from nats to bits.","For \\(x'=kx(1-x)\\) with \\(k=1.2\\) and \\(x(0)=1/4\\), find the log-odds \\(L(0)\\), derive \\(L(t)=L(0)+kt\\), and solve for \\(x(t)\\). Use the binary-dynamics demo to verify that log-odds form a straight line in time even though \\(x(t)\\) is curved. What is the time at which the population first reaches \\(x=1/2\\)?","Investigate \\(x'=x(1-x)(3-4x)\\) starting at \\(x(0)=0.1\\). Use the demo to locate where Shannon entropy increases, reaches a maximum, and decreases; sketch both \\(x(t)\\) and \\(H(x(t))\\). Derive \\(\\dot H=\\ln((1-x)/x)x(1-x)(3-4x)\\) and use signs to explain the changing behavior. Compare the stable equilibrium \\(x^*=3/4\\) with the entropy maximum at \\(x=1/2\\).","For three strategies with constant fitnesses \\(f=(1,0,-1)\\) and initial distribution \\(p(0)=(0.2,0.3,0.5)\\), derive the exponential normalization formula and compute the normalized weights at \\(t=1\\). Verify that the components sum to one. Use the demo's three-strategy mode to compare \\(p(t)\\) and entropy at \\(t=0,1,4\\). Prove that adding the same constant to all three fitnesses does not change the normalized distribution, and explain why the model is different from the frequency-dependent cyclic system of Week 3."],"quiz":[["For an event with probability \\(p\\), surprisal in nats is",["\\(-\\ln p\\)","\\(\\ln p\\)","\\(p\\ln p\\)","\\(1/p\\)"],0,"Surprisal is −ln p, positive for probabilities strictly between zero and one."],["For two independent events, the surprisal of their joint occurrence is",["the product of their surprisals","the sum of their surprisals","always zero","the larger surprisal only"],1,"Independence multiplies probabilities, and the logarithm turns a product into a sum."],["Shannon entropy of \\(P=(1/2,1/2)\\) in bits is",["0 bits","1 bit","2 bits","\\(\\frac12\\) bit"],1,"Each event has surprisal one bit, so the average is one bit."],["Bernoulli Shannon entropy \\(H(p)\\) is maximized at",["\\(p=0\\)","\\(p=1/4\\)","\\(p=1/2\\)","\\(p=1\\)"],2,"H′=ln((1−p)/p) vanishes at p=1/2 and H″ is negative."],["For \\(x'=x(1-x)(f_A-f_B)\\), the derivative of \\(L=\\ln[x/(1-x)]\\) is",["\\(x(1-x)\\)","\\(f_A-f_B\\)","\\(\\ln(x)\\)","\\(H(x)\\)"],1,"Differentiate the log-odds and cancel the factor x(1−x)."],["With a constant fitness difference, what happens to log-odds as time passes?",["They follow a sinusoid","They are constant for any nonzero fitness difference","They change linearly with time","They must be bounded between 0 and 1"],2,"The log-odds derivative is the constant fitness difference."],["For constant fitnesses \\(f_i\\), the replicator solution is",["\\(p_i(t)=p_i(0)+f_it\\)","\\(p_i(t)=\\frac{p_i(0)e^{f_it}}{\\sum_jp_j(0)e^{f_jt}}\\)","\\(p_i(t)=e^{f_it}\\)","\\(p_i(t)=f_i/\\sum_jf_j\\)"],1,"Exponentially grow each initial weight, then normalize the weights to sum to one."],["Along a stable replicator trajectory, Shannon entropy",["always increases","always decreases","can increase and later decrease","must remain equal to its maximum"],2,"Entropy measures uncertainty, while asymptotic stability concerns motion toward an equilibrium."]],"sections":[["Surprisal and Shannon entropy","The surprisal of an outcome with probability \\(p>0\\) is \\(I=-\\ln p\\) nats. For independent events, surprisals add because probabilities multiply. Shannon entropy is the expected surprisal \\(H(\\mathbf p)=-\\sum_i p_i\\ln p_i\\), with \\(0\\ln0=0\\). Entropy in bits uses \\(\\log_2\\) instead of the natural logarithm."],["Binary entropy and calculus","For a Bernoulli distribution, \\(H(x)=-x\\ln x-(1-x)\\ln(1-x)\\), with \\(H'(x)=\\ln((1-x)/x)\\) and \\(H''(x)=-1/[x(1-x)]\\). Its maximum is \\(\\ln2\\) nats at \\(x=1/2\\). For n categories, the uniform distribution maximizes entropy at \\(\\ln n\\) nats."],["Log-odds and the replicator equation","For \\(0<x<1\\), define \\(L=\\ln(x/(1-x))\\). If \\(x'=x(1-x)(f_A-f_B)\\), then \\(L'=x'/[x(1-x)]=f_A-f_B\\). For a constant fitness difference \\(k\\), \\(L(t)=L(0)+kt\\), and \\(x(t)=1/[1+((1-x_0)/x_0)e^{-kt}]\\). Thus a logistic trajectory becomes a straight line in log-odds coordinates."],["Exponential normalization and entropy change","For constant strategy fitnesses \\(f_i\\), \\(p_i(t)=p_i(0)e^{f_it}/\\sum_jp_j(0)e^{f_jt}\\). This is exponential weighting followed by softmax-like normalization. But Shannon entropy need not grow: along \\(x'=x(1-x)\\Delta(x)\\), \\(\\dot H=\\ln((1-x)/x)\\,x(1-x)\\Delta(x)\\), which may change sign during the trajectory."]],"key":"H(\\mathbf p)=-\\sum_i p_i\\ln p_i,\\qquad \\frac{d}{dt}\\ln\\frac{x}{1-x}=f_A-f_B","example":"For \\(x'=x(1-x)(3-4x)\\) with \\(x(0)=0.1\\), the state moves toward \\(3/4\\). Its Shannon entropy first increases as it passes toward \\(1/2\\), reaches its maximum \\(\\ln2\\) there, then decreases on the way to the stable equilibrium. Stability does not require Shannon entropy to increase.","readingPath":"readings/week05.html","demoPath":"demos/week05.html","labPath":"labs/week05.html","quizPath":"quizzes/week05.html"},{"week":6,"title":"Relative Entropy, Stability, and the Geometry Ahead","subtitle":"KL divergence as an equilibrium-adapted Lyapunov function","summary":"Understand KL as excess log loss, derive its monotone decay along stable replicator flows, and identify the quadratic form that leads to Fisher geometry.","reading":"readings/week06.html","demo":"demos/week06.html","lab":["Start with two Bernoulli distributions \\(Q=(3/4,1/4)\\) and \\(P=(1/2,1/2)\\). (a) Calculate \\(D(Q\\|P)\\) and \\(D(P\\|Q)\\) in nats. (b) Explain why they differ even though they compare the same two points. (c) Compute \\(H(Q)\\) and the cross-entropy \\(\\operatorname{CE}(Q,P)\\), then verify \\(\\operatorname{CE}(Q,P)-H(Q)=D(Q\\|P)\\). (d) In the binary demo, compare the two divergences for \\(q=0.75,\\;x=0.5\\).","Prove Gibbs' inequality for strictly positive finite distributions. Begin with \\(\\ln u\\leq u-1\\) and substitute \\(u=p_i/q_i\\). Multiply by \\(q_i\\), sum, and use normalization. State precisely when equality holds. Then describe what happens to \\(D(Q\\|P)\\) when Q assigns positive probability to an outcome that P declares impossible.","For \\(x'=x(1-x)(3-4x)\\), let \\(q=3/4\\) and \\(V(x)=D(q\\|x)\\). (a) Differentiate V with respect to x and simplify to \\((x-q)/[x(1-x)]\\). (b) Apply the chain rule to prove \\(\\dot V=-4(x-q)^2\\). (c) In the binary explorer, start at \\(x(0)=0.1\\). Explain why Shannon entropy first increases then decreases while V always decreases. (d) Relate the derivative calculation to asymptotic stability.","In the categorical explorer, set the reference \\(Q=(0.5,0.3,0.2)\\) and the proposed model \\(P=(0.15,0.20,0.65)\\). (a) Compute \\(D(Q\\|P)\\), \\(D(P\\|Q)\\), and the expected log loss of using P when Q generates observations. (b) Define \\(P(t)=(1-t)P+tQ\\) and evaluate the forward KL divergence at \\(t=0,\\;0.5,\\;1\\). (c) Use the simplex heatmap to interpret the minimum. (d) Explain why this interpolation does not need to solve the replicator equation.","Consider a reference Bernoulli probability q and a small displacement h. (a) Write \\(D(q\\|q+h)\\) and show that its first derivative in h vanishes at 0. (b) Calculate the second derivative at 0 and derive the leading quadratic term. (c) Compute the quadratic approximations for \\(q=0.2\\) and \\(q=0.5\\) with \\(h=0.02\\). Which state produces greater information mismatch for the same probability change? (d) For a three-outcome reference \\(\\mathbf q\\), explain why a tangent displacement must satisfy \\(h_A+h_B+h_C=0\\)."],"quiz":[["KL divergence \\(D(Q\\|P)\\) is the difference between",["cross-entropy CE(Q,P) and Shannon entropy H(Q)","the two entropy values H(Q) and H(P)","two Euclidean lengths","probability and variance"],0,"D(Q||P)=CE(Q,P)−H(Q), the excess expected log loss using P when Q generates observations."],["Which statement about KL divergence is correct?",["It is symmetric for all distributions.","It is never negative but is generally asymmetric.","It always satisfies the triangle inequality.","It equals Shannon entropy."],1,"Gibbs' inequality gives nonnegativity, but reversing its arguments generally changes the value."],["If \\(q_i>0\\) but \\(p_i=0\\) for some outcome, then \\(D(Q\\|P)\\) is",["zero","always less than one","infinite","negative"],2,"The proposed model assigns zero probability to an outcome that can occur under Q."],["For \\(V(x)=D(q\\|x)\\) with interior q, which derivative is correct?",["\\(\\frac{x-q}{x(1-x)}\\)","\\(\\frac{q-x}{x(1-x)}\\)","\\(q\\ln x\\)","\\(x(1-x)\\)"],0,"Differentiate the two logarithmic terms and combine them over x(1−x)."],["For \\(x'=x(1-x)(3-4x)\\) and \\(q=3/4\\), the KL Lyapunov derivative is",["\\(4(x-q)^2\\)","\\(-4(x-q)^2\\)","\\(\\ln(x/q)\\)","\\(0\\) for every x"],1,"Use the chain rule: V′x′=(x−q)(3−4x)=−4(x−q)²."],["Along this stable coexistence solution, Shannon entropy",["must increase at all times","equals KL divergence","can increase then decrease even while KL decreases","is identically zero"],2,"Starting below x=1/2, the entropy rises to ln2 then falls as x approaches q=3/4."],["The leading term of \\(D(q\\|q+h)\\) for small h is",["\\(\\frac{h}{q(1-q)}\\)","\\(\\frac{h^2}{2q(1-q)}\\)","\\(h^2q(1-q)\\)","\\(q+h\\)"],1,"At equality, KL and its first derivative vanish; the second derivative is 1/[q(1−q)]."],["Which statement best describes the connection to Week 7?",["KL itself is a Euclidean distance.","The KL quadratic term defines a tangent-space metric on positive distributions.","All points in the simplex have the same quadratic coefficient.","The metric is unrelated to probabilities."],1,"The local quadratic term is positive definite on tangent vectors and produces Fisher information geometry."]],"sections":[["From entropy to relative entropy","If the actual distribution is \\(Q=(q_i)\\) and the predictive distribution is \\(P=(p_i)\\), the expected logarithmic loss is \\(\\operatorname{CE}(Q,P)=-\\sum_iq_i\\ln p_i\\). Since \\(H(Q)=-\\sum_iq_i\\ln q_i\\), their difference is \\(D(Q\\|P)=\\operatorname{CE}(Q,P)-H(Q)=\\sum_iq_i\\ln(q_i/p_i)\\). The order matters: the generating reference distribution is first. Divergence is nonnegative, but generally asymmetric. If some \\(q_i>0\\) and \\(p_i=0\\), it is infinite."],["Nonnegativity and the simplex","The inequality \\(\\ln u\\leq u-1\\) implies \\(D(Q\\|P)\\geq\\sum_i(q_i-p_i)=0\\) for positive distributions. Equality occurs precisely when the distributions agree. For fixed positive Q, \\(D(Q\\|P)\\) is a scalar landscape over the simplex with its minimum at P=Q."],["A Lyapunov function for a replicator equation","For \\(x'=x(1-x)(a-bx)\\), take \\(b>0\\) and \\(q=a/b\\in(0,1)\\). Let \\(V(x)=D(q\\|x)=q\\ln(q/x)+(1-q)\\ln((1-q)/(1-x))\\). Differentiating gives \\(V'(x)=(x-q)/[x(1-x)]\\) and hence \\(\\dot V=(x-q)(a-bx)=-b(x-q)^2\\leq0\\). This proves the strict decay of KL mismatch away from the attracting equilibrium; it need not coincide with increasing Shannon entropy."],["The local quadratic form","For a small change \\(h\\) around interior Bernoulli state q, Taylor expansion gives \\(D(q\\|q+h)=h^2/[2q(1-q)]+O(h^3)\\). More generally, a small tangent displacement \\(\\mathbf h\\) with \\(\\sum_i h_i=0\\) satisfies \\(D(\\mathbf q\\|\\mathbf q+\\mathbf h)=\\frac12\\sum_i h_i^2/q_i+O(\\|\\mathbf h\\|^3)\\). The quadratic term is the geometric object that becomes the Fisher information metric in Week 7."]],"key":"\\frac{d}{dt}D(q\\|x(t))=-b(x-q)^2,\\qquad D(q\\|q+h)=\\frac{h^2}{2q(1-q)}+O(h^3)","example":"For \\(x'=x(1-x)(3-4x)\\), the equilibrium is \\(q=3/4\\). The function \\(V(x)=\\frac34\\ln[(3/4)/x]+\\frac14\\ln[(1/4)/(1-x)]\\) decreases along every nonstationary interior trajectory, since \\(\\dot V=-4(x-3/4)^2\\). Unlike Shannon entropy, this KL divergence is guaranteed to decay in this model.","readingPath":"readings/week06.html","demoPath":"demos/week06.html","labPath":"labs/week06.html","quizPath":"quizzes/week06.html"},{"week":7,"title":"Fisher Information and Local Geometry","subtitle":"From local KL divergence to lengths, angles, and speeds","summary":"Construct the Fisher metric from KL curvature and score functions, measure Bernoulli lengths and categorical angles, and interpret replicator speed as fitness variance.","reading":"readings/week07.html","demo":"demos/week07.html","lab":["Return to KL divergence. For interior Bernoulli probability p and small displacement h, write \\(D(p\\|p+h)\\). (a) Differentiate once and twice with respect to h, evaluating both derivatives at \\(h=0\\). (b) Explain why the first-order term vanishes and derive \\(ds^2=dp^2/[p(1-p)]\\). (c) Independently derive the same Fisher coefficient as the expected squared Bernoulli score. Give a sentence explaining why the two derivations agree conceptually.","Use the Bernoulli Fisher ruler in the demo. (a) Calculate \\(g(p)\\) at \\(p=0.1,0.25,0.5,0.9\\). (b) Evaluate the exact Fisher distances for the two moves \\(0.10\\to0.15\\) and \\(0.50\\to0.55\\). Compare them with the first-order estimates \\(|dp|/\\sqrt{p(1-p)}\\). (c) Starting with \\(ds^2=dp^2/[p(1-p)]\\), substitute \\(p=\\sin^2(\\theta/2)\\) to prove \\(ds^2=d\\theta^2\\). Why is the total Fisher length of the open Bernoulli interval \\(\\pi\\)?","At \\(q=(0.5,0.3,0.2)\\), consider the tangent vectors \\(u=(0.1,-0.05,-0.05)\\) and \\(v=(0,0.1,-0.1)\\). (a) Verify that both are tangent to the simplex. (b) Compute their Euclidean dot product, the Fisher dot product, their Fisher squared norms, and the Fisher angle. (c) Use the categorical demo to compare these two notions of angle. (d) In independent coordinates \\(x=p_A,y=p_B\\), derive the \\(2\\times2\\) Fisher metric matrix.","Take two strategies with constant fitnesses \\(f_A=2,\\ f_B=0\\) and initial distribution \\(p=(1/4,3/4)\\). (a) Compute mean fitness, fitness variance, and the replicator velocity. (b) Compute \\(\\|\\mathbf p'\\|_F^2\\) directly from the metric and compare it with the variance. (c) Derive the general identity \\(\\|\\mathbf p'\\|_F^2=\\operatorname{Var}_p(f)\\) for three or more strategies. (d) Use the demo to see how speed changes as the trajectory approaches fixation.","Suppose a Bernoulli fitness advantage depends on p as \\(\\Delta(p)=3-4p\\). Define the potential \\(\\Phi(p)=\\int_0^p(3-4s)\\,ds\\). (a) Compute \\(\\Phi(p)\\) and its ordinary derivative. (b) Multiply by the inverse Fisher metric to obtain the natural gradient. (c) Show that its flow reproduces the Week 1 replicator equation. (d) Explain the difference between a critical point of the potential, a Fisher-gradient equilibrium, and a maximum of Shannon entropy."],"quiz":[["For a positive categorical distribution \\(\\mathbf p\\), the Fisher squared norm of a tangent vector u is",["\\(\\sum_i u_i^2/p_i\\)","\\(\\sum_i p_i u_i^2\\)","\\((\\sum_i u_i)^2\\)","\\(\\sum_i u_i\\)"],0,"The local quadratic KL term gives the Fisher inner product weighted by 1/p_i."],["For Bernoulli probability p, the Fisher information coefficient is",["\\(p(1-p)\\)","\\(1/[p(1-p)]\\)","\\(p^2\\)","\\(1\\)"],1,"Evaluate the expected squared Bernoulli score, or the second derivative of KL."],["At \\(p=1/2\\), the Bernoulli Fisher coefficient is",["1","2","4","8"],2,"1/[0.5(1−0.5)]=4."],["Which coordinate makes the Bernoulli Fisher metric equal to \\(d\\theta^2\\)?",["\\(\\theta=p^2\\)","\\(\\theta=\\ln p\\)","\\(\\theta=2\\arcsin\\sqrt p\\)","\\(\\theta=p(1-p)\\)"],2,"The substitution p=sin²(θ/2) cancels the metric coefficient."],["For two monotone Bernoulli states a and b, intrinsic Fisher distance is",["the KL divergence D(a||b)","\\(2|\\arcsin\\sqrt b-\\arcsin\\sqrt a|\\)","\\(|a-b|\\)","\\(1/[a(1-a)]\\)"],1,"Integrate the Fisher line element dp/√[p(1−p)]."],["For a three-category tangent vector u, which constraint holds?",["\\(\\sum_i u_i=0\\)","\\(\\sum_i u_i=1\\)","\\(u_i\\geq0\\) for all i","\\(\\sum_i u_i^2=1\\)"],0,"Differentiate the normalization condition ∑p_i=1."],["Along replicator dynamics, \\(\\|\\mathbf p'\\|_F^2\\) equals",["mean fitness","Shannon entropy","fitness variance","the entropy production rate"],2,"Substituting p_i'=p_i(f_i−mean f) produces the weighted variance of fitness."],["For Bernoulli potential \\(\\Phi(p)\\), its Fisher natural gradient has scalar component",["\\(\\Phi'(p)\\)","\\(p(1-p)\\Phi'(p)\\)","\\(\\Phi'(p)/[p(1-p)]\\)","\\(p+\\Phi'(p)\\)"],1,"The Fisher gradient multiplies the ordinary derivative by the inverse metric g⁻¹=p(1−p)."]],"sections":[["The KL quadratic term defines length","For a positive reference distribution \\(\\mathbf p\\), a small tangent displacement \\(\\mathbf h\\) with \\(\\sum_i h_i=0\\) obeys \\(D(\\mathbf p\\|\\mathbf p+\\mathbf h)=\\frac12\\sum_i h_i^2/p_i+O(\\|\\mathbf h\\|^3)\\). Thus the Fisher inner product on tangent vectors is \\(g_{\\mathbf p}(\\mathbf u,\\mathbf v)=\\sum_i u_iv_i/p_i\\). It measures local distinguishability, not a global KL distance."],["Bernoulli information and intrinsic distance","For \\(P_p=(p,1-p)\\), Fisher information equals \\(I(p)=E_p[(\\partial_p\\ln P_p(X))^2]=1/[p(1-p)]\\). Hence \\(ds^2=dp^2/[p(1-p)]\\), and the arc coordinate \\(\\theta=2\\arcsin\\sqrt p\\) satisfies \\(ds^2=d\\theta^2\\). Consequently \\(d_F(a,b)=2|\\arcsin\\sqrt b-\\arcsin\\sqrt a|\\). The metric blows up at the coordinate boundaries, but the distance to a boundary is finite."],["Lengths and angles in the categorical simplex","For \\(\\mathbf p=(x,y,1-x-y)\\), a tangent vector obeys \\(h_1+h_2+h_3=0\\), so the metric matrix in x,y coordinates is \\(G(x,y)=\\begin{pmatrix}1/x+1/z&1/z\\\\1/z&1/y+1/z\\end{pmatrix}\\) with \\(z=1-x-y\\). Two tangent vectors need not have the same Fisher angle as their Euclidean angle; use \\(\\cos\\alpha_F=g_p(u,v)/[\\|u\\|_F\\|v\\|_F]\\)."],["Replicator speed and the natural gradient","The squared Fisher speed of an interior probability curve is \\(\\sum_i(p_i')^2/p_i\\). Along replicator dynamics \\(p_i'=p_i(f_i-\\bar f)\\), this becomes \\(\\sum_i p_i(f_i-\\bar f)^2=\\operatorname{Var}_{\\mathbf p}(f)\\). For Bernoulli, the Fisher gradient of a potential \\(\\Phi\\) is \\(p(1-p)\\Phi'(p)\\), so a constant fitness advantage gives the logistic equation as a Fisher gradient flow."]],"key":"g_{\\mathbf p}(u,v)=\\sum_i\\frac{u_iv_i}{p_i},\\qquad ds^2=\\frac{dp^2}{p(1-p)},\\qquad \\|\\mathbf p'\\|_F^2=\\operatorname{Var}_{\\mathbf p}(f)","example":"At Bernoulli probability \\(p=1/2\\), the Fisher coefficient is 4; at \\(p=1/10\\), it is \\(100/9\\approx11.11\\). The Fisher distance from \\(a\\) to \\(b\\) is \\(2|\\arcsin\\sqrt b-\\arcsin\\sqrt a|\\). For replicator fitnesses \\(f=(2,0)\\) and \\(p=(1/4,3/4)\\), both fitness variance and squared Fisher speed are \\(3/4\\).","readingPath":"readings/week07.html","demoPath":"demos/week07.html","labPath":"labs/week07.html","quizPath":"quizzes/week07.html"},{"week":8,"title":"Square-Root Coordinates and Spherical Geometry","subtitle":"The simplex becomes a sphere","summary":"Compute Fisher arc length and geodesic distance using elementary surface geometry.","reading":"readings/week08.html","demo":"demos/week08.html","lab":["Prove that \\(q_i=2\\sqrt{p_i}\\) sends normalized probability vectors to a sphere of radius 2.","Use differentials to show that the Fisher quadratic form becomes ordinary Euclidean length on the sphere.","Compute the Fisher distance between Bernoulli states \\(p=1/4\\) and \\(p=3/4\\) using \\(2\\arcsin\\sqrt p\\).","Use the simplex/sphere demo to compare two distributions. Find a pair that is close in ordinary Euclidean coordinates but noticeably separated in Fisher distance."],"quiz":[["Under (q_i=2sqrt{p_i}), the simplex maps to part of a sphere of radius",["1","2","4","(sqrt2)"],1,"The squared coordinates sum to (4)."],["The square-root map transforms the Fisher metric into",["the Euclidean metric restricted to the sphere","an indefinite metric","the taxicab metric","zero"],0,"Each (dq_i^2=dp_i^2/p_i)."],["Shortest spherical paths lie on",["latitude circles always","great circles","straight chords through the ball","spirals"],1,"Intrinsic geodesics on a round sphere are great-circle arcs."],["The Fisher distance between the two pure Bernoulli distributions (in the completion) is",["(0)","(1)","(pi)","infinite"],2,"The corresponding radius-2 vectors make a right angle."],["The Fisher metric boundary of the categorical simplex is",["infinitely far away","at finite metric distance","not definable as a limit","Euclidean-flat everywhere"],1,"The square-root sphere has a finite arc to the boundary."]],"sections":[["A surprising change of variables","On the categorical simplex, tangent vectors have component sum zero and the Fisher metric is \\(ds^2=\\sum_i dp_i^2/p_i\\). Set \\(q_i=2\\sqrt{p_i}\\). Then \\(dq_i=dp_i/\\sqrt{p_i}\\), so \\(ds^2=\\sum_i dq_i^2\\). Normalization becomes \\(\\sum_i q_i^2=4\\). The simplex interior is therefore isometric to the positive orthant of a sphere of radius 2."],["Length and shortest paths","On a sphere of radius 2, shortest paths are arcs of great circles. The angle between square-root vectors \\(\\mathbf q\\) and \\(\\mathbf r\\) is determined by their dot product. Translating back to probabilities,\n\\[\nd_F(\\mathbf p,\\mathbf r)=2\\arccos\\left(\\sum_i\\sqrt{p_ir_i}\\right).\n\\]\nThe quantity inside arccos is called the Bhattacharyya coefficient. It lies between 0 and 1 for probability vectors."],["Bernoulli arc length","For two outcomes, let \\(\\theta(p)=2\\arcsin\\sqrt p\\). Differentiation gives \\(d\\theta=dp/\\sqrt{p(1-p)}\\). Thus the Fisher distance between Bernoulli parameters is \\(|\\theta(p)-\\theta(r)|\\). Although the coordinate metric coefficient diverges toward the boundary, the boundary itself lies at finite Fisher distance."]],"key":"q_i=2\\sqrt{p_i},\\qquad d_F(p,r)=2\\arccos\\sum_i\\sqrt{p_ir_i}","example":"The two pure Bernoulli distributions \\((1,0)\\) and \\((0,1)\\), viewed in the metric completion, are separated by Fisher distance \\(\\pi\\): their square-root vectors are orthogonal, subtending angle \\(\\pi/2\\) on a sphere of radius 2.","readingPath":"readings/week08.html","demoPath":"demos/week08.html","labPath":"labs/week08.html","quizPath":"quizzes/week08.html"},{"week":9,"title":"Natural Gradients and Potential-Driven Evolution","subtitle":"Returning to our first ODE","summary":"Interpret potential replicator dynamics as Fisher gradient flows and distinguish nongradient motion.","reading":"readings/week09.html","demo":"demos/week09.html","lab":["For a one-dimensional metric \\(ds^2=g(x)\\,dx^2\\), derive why the gradient component is \\(V'/g\\).","Let \\(V(x)=2x-3x^2/2\\). Write its Fisher gradient flow and find the attracting interior equilibrium.","For \\(V(p_1,p_2,p_3)=2p_1+p_2\\), compute the Fisher gradient at \\(p=(1/3,1/3,1/3)\\). Verify the components sum to zero.","Compare the natural-gradient demo with the cyclic competition demo from Week 3. Describe evidence that the second system cannot be a strictly increasing gradient flow on closed orbits."],"quiz":[["For (ds^2=g(x)dx^2), the metric gradient of V has component",["(gV')","(V'/g)","(V'')","(V)"],1,"The metric converts the derivative covector to a vector using its inverse."],["The Bernoulli natural gradient multiplier is",["(x(1-x))","(1/[x(1-x)])","(x+1)","(1)"],0,"It is the inverse of the Fisher coefficient."],["In a potential replicator system, fitnesses have the form",["(f_i=0)","(f_i=partial_i V)","(f_i=p_i) always","(f_i=ln p_i) always"],1,"These are the components of the ordinary derivative of the potential."],["Along an upward Fisher gradient flow, (dV/dt) is",["nonnegative","always negative","undefined","constant -1"],0,"It equals the squared metric norm of the gradient."],["Which phenomenon rules out a strictly increasing potential along a nonstationary trajectory?",["convergence to an equilibrium","a closed orbit","a local minimum","normalization"],1,"A strictly increasing scalar function cannot return to its starting value."]],"sections":[["The gradient depends on the metric","In ordinary Euclidean coordinates, the gradient of \\(V(x)\\) is \\(V'(x)\\). If length is instead measured by \\(ds^2=g(x)\\,dx^2\\), the metric gradient is \\(\\operatorname{grad}_gV=g(x)^{-1}V'(x)\\partial_x\\). For Bernoulli Fisher geometry, \\(g^{-1}=x(1-x)\\). Hence the scalar replicator equation \\(x'=x(1-x)V'(x)\\) is a Fisher gradient flow whenever the fitness difference equals \\(V'(x)\\)."],["The multivariable formula","For a potential \\(V(\\mathbf p)\\) on the simplex, the Fisher natural gradient has components\n\\[\n(\\operatorname{grad}_F V)_i=p_i\\left(\\frac{\\partial V}{\\partial p_i}-\\sum_jp_j\\frac{\\partial V}{\\partial p_j}\\right).\n\\]\nThe subtraction ensures that the components sum to zero. If the fitnesses are \\(f_i=\\partial_i V\\), this is precisely the replicator equation. Along its flow,\n\\[\n\\frac{dV}{dt}=g(\\operatorname{grad}_FV,\\operatorname{grad}_FV)\\geq0.\n\\]"],["An important limitation","Not every replicator equation admits a global potential. Cyclic games can produce closed orbits or rotational behavior, impossible for a strictly increasing potential along nonstationary closed trajectories. The Fisher metric is useful in both cases; only the special potential games are natural-gradient flows. This distinction prevents us from interpreting every equilibrium as the solution of an optimization problem."]],"key":"(\\operatorname{grad}_F V)_i=p_i\\left(\\partial_iV-\\sum_jp_j\\partial_jV\\right)","example":"Choose \\(V(x)=3x-2x^2\\). Then \\(V'=3-4x\\) and \\(\\operatorname{grad}_F V=x(1-x)(3-4x)\\partial_x\\), the very ODE we first analyzed.","readingPath":"readings/week09.html","demoPath":"demos/week09.html","labPath":"labs/week09.html","quizPath":"quizzes/week09.html"},{"week":10,"title":"Gaussian Families and Hyperbolic Geometry","subtitle":"The geometry of familiar statistical models","summary":"Calculate the Fisher matrix for normal distributions and compare spherical and hyperbolic statistical geometry.","reading":"readings/week10.html","demo":"demos/week10.html","lab":["Differentiate the Gaussian log-density with respect to \\(\\mu\\) and \\(\\sigma\\). Check the units of both derivatives.","Use the moments \\(E[(Z-\\mu)^2]=\\sigma^2\\) and \\(E[(Z-\\mu)^4]=3\\sigma^4\\) to compute both diagonal entries of the Fisher matrix.","Set \\(v=\\sqrt2\\,\\sigma\\). Transform the metric to \\(2(du^2+dv^2)/v^2\\). What geometric model does this identify?","Use the demo to compare the same change in mean at small and large standard deviation. Explain the conclusion using the Fisher metric. Then contrast this hyperbolic geometry with the spherical categorical simplex."],"quiz":[["The Gaussian location-scale family uses parameters",["((mu,sigma)), (sigma>0)","p only","integers only","three categorical weights"],0,"The mean and positive standard deviation specify the law."],["The Fisher metric coefficient of (dmu^2) is",["(sigma^2)","(1/sigma^2)","(2sigma^2)","1"],1,"The squared mean score has expectation (1/sigma^2)."],["The Fisher metric coefficient of (dsigma^2) is",["(2/sigma^2)","(1/sigma)","(sigma^2)","0"],0,"The fourth centered moment gives the factor 2."],["After rescaling coordinates, Gaussian information geometry is",["spherical","hyperbolic","Euclidean with constant coefficients","discrete"],1,"The metric is a multiple of the upper half-plane metric."],["Which statistical space has positive constant curvature in our course?",["Gaussian location-scale family","categorical simplex with Fisher metric","both","neither"],1,"The square-root simplex is a part of a radius-2 sphere."]],"sections":[["A two-parameter family","Consider the normal density\n\\[\np(z;\\mu,\\sigma)=\\frac{1}{\\sigma\\sqrt{2\\pi}}\\exp\\left(-\\frac{(z-\\mu)^2}{2\\sigma^2}\\right),\\quad \\sigma>0.\n\\]\nThe point \\((\\mu,\\sigma)\\) specifies a probability distribution. Thus the upper half-plane is a parameter space whose geometry we can compute from changes of its densities."],["Compute the Fisher matrix","Differentiate the log-density:\n\\[\n\\partial_\\mu\\ln p=\\frac{z-\\mu}{\\sigma^2},\\qquad\n\\partial_\\sigma\\ln p=-\\frac1\\sigma+\\frac{(z-\\mu)^2}{\\sigma^3}.\n\\]\nTaking expectations under the same normal distribution gives \\(E[(z-\\mu)^2]=\\sigma^2\\) and \\(E[(z-\\mu)^4]=3\\sigma^4\\). The cross term vanishes, and\n\\[\nG(\\mu,\\sigma)=\\begin{pmatrix}\\sigma^{-2}&0\\\\0&2\\sigma^{-2}\\end{pmatrix},\n\\qquad ds^2=\\frac{d\\mu^2+2d\\sigma^2}{\\sigma^2}.\n\\]"],["From the half-plane to hyperbolic geometry","Let \\(u=\\mu\\) and \\(v=\\sqrt2\\,\\sigma\\). Then \\(ds^2=2(du^2+dv^2)/v^2\\), a constant multiple of the Poincaré half-plane metric. Unlike the spherical categorical model, the Gaussian location-scale family has constant negative Gaussian curvature \\(-1/2\\). Its intrinsic geodesics are vertical rays and semicircular arcs orthogonal to the line \\(v=0\\)."],["Closing the loop","We began with a curve of population proportions governed by differential equations. We recognized probability distributions as points of a space, learned to measure local distinguishability, and discovered familiar geometric surfaces hidden inside statistical models. Differential equations motivated the geometry; the geometry, in turn, explained the particular form of potential-driven replicator equations."]],"key":"ds^2_{\\mathrm{Gaussian}}=\\frac{d\\mu^2+2\\,d\\sigma^2}{\\sigma^2}","example":"Near \\((\\mu,\\sigma)=(0,1)\\), a small change \\(d\\mu=0.1\\), \\(d\\sigma=0\\) has approximate Fisher length \\(0.1\\). At \\((0,2)\\) the same mean change has approximate length \\(0.05\\); a broad distribution distinguishes shifts less sharply.","readingPath":"readings/week10.html","demoPath":"demos/week10.html","labPath":"labs/week10.html","quizPath":"quizzes/week10.html"}];
